Q: Why doesn’t JavaScript provide a native way to cancel Promise.all when one promise fails? A: <p>It seems there's a misunderstanding on what <code>Promise.all</code> does and perhaps even what a <code>Promise</code> is. <code>Promise.all</code> doesn't <em>execute</em> the promises.  It's only used to await <em>all</em> the promises in a list of them. It creates <em>a new promise</em> from the list of <em>all</em> promises, that completes when either all succeed or at least one fails. A <code>Promise</code> itself <em>represents</em> an asynchronous operation, it's not the operation itself, or a thread.</p>
<p>A more appropriate question would be <em>What is the native way to cancel asynchronous operations in Javascript</em> ?</p>
<p>First, as the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Promise" rel="nofollow noreferrer">MDN doc page on Promise</a> explains :</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Promise itself has no first-class protocol for cancellation, but you may be able to directly cancel the underlying asynchronous operation, typically using <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortController" rel="nofollow noreferrer">AbortController</a>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><code>fetch</code> already <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/RequestInit#signal" rel="nofollow noreferrer">accepts an AbortSignal</a> created by AbortController, <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortSignal#examples" rel="nofollow noreferrer">as the first example in the doc page</a> shows :</p>
<pre><code>async function fetchVideo() {
  controller = new AbortController();
  const signal = controller.signal;

  try {
    const response = await fetch(url, { signal });
    console.log(&quot;Download complete&quot;, response);
    // process response further
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(`Download error: ${err.message}`);
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Asking for cancellation doesn't mean aborting the asynchronous operation. <em>The operation itself</em> checks the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/AbortSignal" rel="nofollow noreferrer">AbortSignal</a>'s status and either exit gracefully or throws. This allows the operation to clean up any resources, close connections etc. It also allows <em>propagating</em> the cancellation to any nested async operations, just by passing them the AbortSignal.</p>
<p>This is a common pattern used in many languages, not just JavaScript:</p>
<ul>
<li>A Promise/Future/Task abstraction represents the asynchronous operation.</li>
<li>The abstractions cancel gracefully using a cancellation source/signal.</li>
<li>Cancellation is triggered by a <em>source</em> that's <em>not</em> directly related to Promise etc.</li>
<li>Operations interested in graceful cancellation (not just async operations) check a Signal/Token generated by that source, not by inspecting the source itself.</li>
<li>When operations detect the cancellation, they either exit gracefully or raise an exception themselves.</li>
</ul>
<p>The docs include one example of graceful cancellation, that doesn't even use promises :</p>
<pre><code>const signal = controller.signal;

// …

if (signal.aborted) {
  if (signal.reason) {
    console.log(`Request aborted with reason: ${signal.reason}`);
  } else {
    console.log(&quot;Request aborted but no reason was given.&quot;);
  }
} else {
  console.log(&quot;Request not aborted&quot;);
}
</code></pre>
